  {
   "id": "8qw1_oxCFgAJ",
   "from": "Perry de Valpine",
   "author": "dd632bd3bbcdd7e9",
   "date": "2019-01-11T23:59:42Z",
   "subject": "Re: Global variables in nimbleFunction",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi Joe,<div><br></div><div>No, there isn&#39;t a system for global variables.  Your approach is very reasonable.</div><div><br></div><div>Another way to do it would be to have MEANINGOFLIFE passed as an argument to setup code.  If it is then used in run code, the compiler will make it be member data in the under-the-hood C++ class.  A variant on this idea would be to create a nimbleFunction with setup code whose only purpose really is to hold the MEANINGOFLIFE value.  An object of this nimbleFunction could then be passed to setup code of other nimbleFunctions, and they will all access the same object, hence the same single MEANINGOFLIFE value.  However, these approaches will not work if the nimbleFunctions you are writing are to be used in models (as user-defined distributions or model functions), because those can&#39;t have setup code.</div><div><br></div><div>After thinking through these options, your solution seems perhaps easiest of all.</div><div><br></div><div>-Perry</div><div><br></div></div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"ltr\">On Wed, Jan 9, 2019 at 2:34 AM &#39;Joseph Chipperfield&#39; via nimble-users &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">nimble...@googlegroups.com</a>&gt; wrote:<br></div></div>"
    ],
